Lab 9 - Service Management Best Practices
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Implement best practices for starting, stopping, and monitoring OpenStack control plane services.
Use systemd to manage services effectively.
Monitor services using openstack-status and systemctl.
Perform rolling upgrades with minimal disruption in a high-availability environment.
Prerequisites
A working OpenStack deployment (e.g., DevStack or multi-node OpenStack).
SSH access to the control plane nodes (controller nodes).
Sudo/root privileges on the nodes.
Basic familiarity with Linux command line and systemd.
Task 1: Managing Control Plane Services with systemd
Subtask 1.1: Listing and Verifying Services
List all OpenStack services managed by systemd:

sudo systemctl list-units --type=service | grep openstack
Expected Output: A list of active/inactive OpenStack services (e.g., nova-api, neutron-server).
Check the status of a specific service (e.g., nova-api):

sudo systemctl status nova-api
Key Concept: systemctl status shows runtime status, logs, and dependencies.
Subtask 1.2: Starting and Stopping Services
Stop a service (e.g., neutron-server):

sudo systemctl stop neutron-server
Verification:
sudo systemctl is-active neutron-server
Expected Output: inactive.
Start the service again:

sudo systemctl start neutron-server
Troubleshooting Tip: If a service fails to start, check logs with:
journalctl -u neutron-server -xe
Task 2: Monitoring Services
Subtask 2.1: Using openstack-status
Install openstack-status (if not available):

sudo apt install openstack-status  # Debian/Ubuntu
sudo yum install openstack-utils  # RHEL/CentOS
Check overall OpenStack service health:

sudo openstack-status
Expected Output: Status of all OpenStack components (e.g., nova: active).
Subtask 2.2: Advanced Monitoring with systemctl
List failed services:

sudo systemctl --failed
Action: Restart failed services using sudo systemctl restart <service>.
Monitor logs in real-time (e.g., for cinder-volume):

sudo journalctl -fu cinder-volume
Key Concept: -f streams logs; -u filters by unit.
Task 3: Rolling Upgrades for High Availability
Subtask 3.1: Prepare for Upgrade
Disable HAProxy on one controller node (to prevent traffic routing):

sudo systemctl stop haproxy
Verify no active connections:

sudo netstat -tulnp | grep haproxy
Expected Output: No output (service is stopped).
Subtask 3.2: Upgrade Services
Upgrade packages (example for Nova):

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade nova-common  # Debian/Ubuntu
sudo yum update openstack-nova-common           # RHEL/CentOS
Restart services sequentially:

sudo systemctl restart nova-api nova-scheduler nova-conductor
Best Practice: Restart services one by one to avoid downtime.
Re-enable HAProxy:

sudo systemctl start haproxy
Subtask 3.3: Verify Cluster Health
Check service synchronization:
openstack compute service list
Expected Output: All services show up state.
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Managed OpenStack services using systemd commands.
Monitored services with openstack-status and journalctl.
Performed a rolling upgrade to minimize downtime.
Key Takeaways:

Always verify service status after changes.
Use journalctl for debugging service failures.
Rolling upgrades require careful sequencing to maintain HA.
